Confirmed: the citation label must carry the test name, and there's a `--fix` mode (`spec:names:fix`) that repairs line numbers BY NAME. That's this repo's reanchor equivalent! `node scripts/check-spec-anchor-names.mjs --fix` repoints each line from the name it carries. That's exactly the sanctioned reanchor flow: after prettier, run `npm run spec:names:fix` (equivalent of reanchor), which moves each #Lnn to its test's current line, by test title, and prints what it could not map.

So my flow:
1. Make all code/test edits, with the new citation label = exact test title and a best-guess #Lnn.
2. prettier the changed .ts files.
3. `node scripts/check-spec-anchor-names.mjs --fix` (repoint by name) — handles ALL anchors in both spec files whose labels match test titles. 
4. Verify no unmap printed.

Beautiful — this makes line bookkeeping trivial and safe. But careful: `--fix` repoints by NAME — if two tests share a title, ambiguity; mine are unique.

Also note: the label format in the spec is `[validated by: <test name>](path#Lnn)` and my new statement gets exactly my test's name.

Now — does `spec:names:fix` also validate that the anchor line lands on a declaration? It resolves by name, so it lands right. 

Next: the `it(` title. Existing titles in the acceptance file:
1. "answers list_sessions for the authenticated user with ISO dates, forwarding limit and before while ignoring any client userId"
2. "answers an empty session_list when the store has no listFor, and SERVER_ERROR with a ws error log when listFor throws"
3. "accepts a bare list_sessions frame and rejects a malformed limit or before with INVALID_MESSAGE without calling listFor"

My new test (in listSessions.test.ts — unit file): "answers a list_sessions asked mid-turn before that turn commits or ends, without waiting on the lock".

Hmm wait — reconsider placement once more. dod.md facet: "A test that a `list_sessions` sent while a `user_message` is streaming is answered before that turn's `stream_end`, so the off-lock claim in protocol spec § 4.3/§ 5.9 carries a citation instead of sitting in the spec-link backlog report."

"before that turn's stream_end" — my unit test asserts session_list is the 4th frame and stream_end comes last — exactly that. The unit harness is right: it isolates the handler's dispatch decision (lock or not), deterministic, and runnable in this pod. ✓

Also §4.3 vs §5.9: dod says "the off-lock claim in protocol spec § 4.3/§ 5.9" — hmm, §4.3 in the CURRENT spec... let me search the spec for other uncited off-lock statements. From the diff, §4.3's statements are all cited; §5.9 bullet 2 is uncited. Also maybe there's an off-lock claim in §4.3's intro paragraph: "The server answers with `session_list` (Section 5.9)." — no off-lock claim. Wait, dod.md says "§ 4.3/§ 5.9" — the previous round's dod facet text. The one uncited statement is §5.9 bullet 2. Hmm — but let me double check §4.3's intro paragraph: is a paragraph a "statement" needing citation? It cites nothing... paragraphs vs bullets — the corpus convention: statements are the bullets; paragraphs sometimes carry citations too (the INVALID_MESSAGE paragraph in 4.3 has one). The 4.3 intro has none. Is the intro paragraph testable? "Asks for the conversations the connection's authenticated user holds... The frame is validated whether or not the store behind the engine can answer it" — those are covered by existing citations on the bullets. I'll cite §5.9 bullet 2 only. If check-spec-status flags a leftover unlinked statement... it's a report, not a gate for specs? "the statement-link report behind --coverage is never a gate". And require-statement-links is deliberately absent from the blocking config. So bullets uncited are allowed (backlog). My citation clears the named facet. ✓

Now — write the code. Let me also double check `check-changelog` won't demand a CHANGELOG entry for this round: check-changelog.sh — probably checks that CHANGELOG has an Unreleased entry matching version bump; previous round already added one. Test-only changes don't need changelog entries. ✓

Let me also peek at how `dod.md` should be updated at the end: tick the last facet.

Also: the ticket's `npm run docs:fix` / full-config items — the previous round did NOT do those (no example/full-config.ts or docs changes in 243e7e5). Those facets belong to later rounds (config flag isn't even built — note the branch went with `list_sessions` always-on; the ticket's opt-in `transport.history` is... hmm, wait. dod.md's facets don't mention the opt-in flag at all. The branch's dod is the brief; the ticket's "opt-in" criterion isn't among its facets. Not my call this round — my round is: CI fixes + the off-lock facet. I'll note remaining gaps in the final message.

Now write the changes.

### 1. listSessions.test.ts — typecheck fix + new test

New file content plan:

```ts
import {WebSocket} from 'ws';
import {createMessageHandler} from './messageHandler.js';
import type {ChatOrchestrator} from '../../orchestration/chatOrchestrator.js';
